Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 23:42:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH v5 00/15] KVM: arm64: FEAT_HDBSS support for stage-2 dirty
 tracking

This series implements hardware-assisted stage-2 dirty tracking on
arm64 using FEAT_HDBSS, and is the combined series of Leonardo Bras'
HAFDBS descriptors rework [1] and the HDBSS buffer support built on
top of it, as requested during the review of [1].

The first five patches form the HAFDBS base: four from Leonardo's
RFC, reworked per the review feedback, plus the folio-account harvest
developed while validating the HDBSS work on top. The descriptor
encoding moves the stage-2 write permission from S2AP[1] to DBM and
reuses S2AP[1] as the dirty state, giving the three states the rest
of the series builds on:

    RO  (DBM=0, S2AP[1]=0)  read-only, write -> permission fault
    WC  (DBM=1, S2AP[1]=0)  writable-clean, hw promotes on write
    WD  (DBM=1, S2AP[1]=1)  writable-dirty

The remaining patches add the HDBSS buffer machinery: per-vCPU
buffers, the buffer-full fault handling, draining the buffers into
the dirty bitmap or the dirty ring on every VM exit, and a single
derived hardware-dirty mode so that:

    - migration with HDBSS  -> HD|HA|HDBSS, hardware tracks dirtiness
    - migration without     -> HD off, classic write-protect faults
    - no migration          -> HD|HA, only written pages go dirty

HAFDBS is not gated on nested virtualization, as shadow stage-2 MMUs
build their own VTCR through kvm_get_vtcr(), which never carries the
HD bit. HDBSS stays gated for now, as the nested exit-flush and
harvest paths are unaudited.

The HDBSS buffer registers are programmed on buffer ownership rather
than on feature enablement (patch 7): HDBSS can be turned on while a
vCPU is mid-KVM_RUN, and hardware dirty-state updates write to
HDBSSBR_EL2.BADDR without any fault, so the registers must already
point at the running vCPU's buffer by then. A 4K dirty-ring
migration validated this under a redis workload.

The KVM_CAP_ARM_HDBSS_BUFFER_SIZE interface is exercised by a new
selftest (patch 15), including the contract that dirty-ring mode
pins the buffer size to the default. Performance numbers for the
non-migration HAFDBS path are being collected and will follow up.

Comments welcome, especially on:

  - the ownership-based HDBSS register programming (patch 7) vs.
    programming them at each VTCR-flipping site,
  - keeping HDBSS NV-gated while HAFDBS is not (patch 12),
  - the per-MMU kvm_hw_dirty_enabled() predicate (patch 12), which
    keeps shadow installs writable-dirty so nested behaviour is
    unchanged.

Relative to Leonardo's RFC [1]: patch 1 keeps reading stage-2
writability from S2AP[1] in the nested walker, as DBM is RES0 from
L1's perspective; patch 2 splits the two dirty ledgers on the fault
paths, the host folio account marked speculatively on PROT_W and the
KVM dirty bitmap only on PROT_DIRTY; his "Enable HAFDBS for guests
not on migration" is replaced by the derived mode (patch 12). The
folio-account harvest, the dirty-ring reservation, and the
buffer-size UAPI with documentation and a selftest are new.

Changes since v4 [2]:

- Rebased onto Leonardo's descriptor rework [1]: the write permission
  moves to DBM, S2AP[1] becomes the pure dirty state, with
  KVM_PGTABLE_PROT_DIRTY, a dedicated write-protect walker and
  KVM_REQ_RELOAD_STAGE2. This replaces v4's auto-DBM patch and drops
  the eager-splitting dependency, as the walker clears DBM on blocks
  so lazy splitting keeps working.

- New: harvest of the stage-2 dirty state into the host folio
  account at unmap/write-protect time, as guest writes bypass the
  host stage-1.

- Buffer lifetime reworked: allocated at vCPU creation, freed at
  destruction, registers programmed on ownership, closing the
  use-after-free window on a concurrent mode switch.

- The auto enable/disable is replaced by a single derived mode, a
  pure function of the capabilities and the number of logging
  memslots: no illegal intermediate VTCR_EL2 state, no locking.

- The buffer flush and the HDBSS fault handling are split into
  separate patches, with the flush at a single point in the run
  loop, before the exit is handled.

- New: dirty ring mode support, with the ring reserving room for a
  full buffer flush and the buffer size pinned to PAGE_SIZE, and the
  KVM_CAP_ARM_HDBSS_BUFFER_SIZE UAPI with documentation and a
  selftest.

